body:
```html
<style>
fieldset {
  display: flex;
  flex-flow: column;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 100px;
  min-height: 200px;
  padding: 0px;
  border: 1px solid;
}

.item {
  height: 100px;
  width: 100px;
  background: orange;
}
</style>

<fieldset>
  <div class="item">
  </div>
</fieldset>
```
(See https://jsfiddle.net/dgrogan/h95vb7gm/4 and https://jsfiddle.net/dgrogan/h95vb7gm/5)

Chrome puts the orange item at the top of the fieldset box. Safari and Firefox center it. I think Chrome's rendering is to spec, but authors seem to [prefer](https://crbug.com/1229458) the Safari/Firefox behavior.

Chrome _appears_ to disobey `justify-content: center` because the anonymous fieldset content box doesn't have a definite `height` or `min-height`, so there is no extra height to center the item in -- the height of the anonymous content box is precisely the height of the item.

That's because [the spec describing the anonymous fieldset content box says](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#anonymous-fieldset-content-box)
**The 'block-size' property is expected to be set to '100%'.**

In this example, the height of the fieldset box is indefinite, so the 100% has nothing to resolve against, so the height of the anonymous box just fits its content.

**Question**: should we change the spec to dictate the Safari/Firefox behavior? I think so, because the Chrome behavior is surprising: the above markup looks like the fieldset IS a flexbox, which would leave the item centered. Authors don't care about the anonymous boxes our specs construct.

If we were to change the spec, we'd have to replace the 100% block-size requirement. Instead, we'd have to have something that dictates:
1. the block size of the anonymous fieldset content box to stretch to fill its parent box at a minimum
2. the anonymous fieldset content box's block size will accommodate its content (a la tables)
3. the resulting block size is definite?
